<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trajan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">53–117 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trajan was Roman emperor from 98 to 117 CE, under whom the empire reached its greatest territorial extent. He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a> in two wars and later campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>, annexing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> for a time. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> discuss him in the context of its military history, and his reign is traditionally regarded as one of the high points of the Roman principate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trajan.jpg" alt="Trajan" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traianus_Glyptothek_Munich_72.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Local government rested on the cities, which were steadily promoted from native communities to Roman municipia and colonies, especially under <b>Trajan</b>, Hadrian and the Severans; many towns kept Punic-style magistrates called sufetes into the second century before adopting Roman offices.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Roman supremacy it kept the status of a free city with its own laws, though it paid tribute, and Pliny the Younger's correspondence with <b>Trajan</b> later shows Roman governors scrutinising its finances.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Samuel the western Bulgarians defeated Basil II at the Gates of <b>Trajan</b> in 986, but were crushed at Kleidion in 1014.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…would rule Armenia as Roman clients, and in 66 CE Nero crowned Tiridates I. The Arsacid kingdom survived <b>Trajan</b>'s annexation of 114–118, the Sasanian occupation of 252 and the restoration of Tiridates III in 287, adopted Christianity, and was partitioned between the Eastern Roman and Sasanian empires in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Elsewhere the bridge at Alcántara over the Tagus, built under <b>Trajan</b> around 104–106 by the architect Gaius Julius Lacer and paid for by Lusitanian communities named on its inscription, still carries traffic, while the temple at Évora and the ruins of Conimbriga are major monuments of Portugal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Repeated Roman invasions, in which <b>Trajan</b> (116 CE), Avidius Cassius (165 CE) and Septimius Severus (198 CE) each captured Ctesiphon, strained resources and damaged royal prestige.</blockquote><a href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italica">Italica</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Selinus, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.44,-6.05&amp;year=94" aria-label="Italica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4399 816 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Trajan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4849 1086h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4849 1086h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.44,-6.05&amp;year=94">What was here in 94 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is not possible to lay down any general rule for all such cases. Do not go out of your way to look for them. If indeed they should be brought before you, and the crime is proved, they must be punished…&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Pliny the Younger (Pliny, Letters, book 10, letter 97) &#183; tr. William Melmoth, rev. F. C. T. Bosanquet, 1915 &#183; his reply on the Christians of Bithynia &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2811/pg2811-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Anonymous informations ought not to be received in any sort of prosecution. It is introducing a very dangerous precedent, and is quite foreign to the spirit of our age.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Pliny the Younger (Pliny, Letters, book 10, letter 97) &#183; tr. William Melmoth, rev. F. C. T. Bosanquet, 1915 &#183; his reply on the Christians of Bithynia &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2811/pg2811-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Whatever name we give them, and for whatever purposes they may be founded, they will not fail to form themselves into factious assemblies, however short their meetings may be.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Pliny the Younger (Pliny, Letters, book 10, letter 34) &#183; tr. William Melmoth, rev. F. C. T. Bosanquet, 1915 &#183; refusing Nicomedia a company of firemen &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2811/pg2811-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
